# modules.cpp SPI flash

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other specifications. This document specifies mm.spiflash,
an SPI NOR flash chip of the Winbond W25Q family as an mm.fs FlashDevice. It
exists and is tested on Linux against the emulated chip of
docs/modules-linux-storage.mdy. No board binds a chip yet, and behaviour on a
real chip remains a hardware-qualification requirement.

docs/modules-fs.mdy owns the FlashDevice seam and mm.fs.littlefs, which mounts
what this module presents. docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns the SPI and
GPIO facilities it drives.

# mm.spiflash

Opening fence
namespace mm::spiflash {

struct Wiring {
    mm::mcu::SpiConfiguration spi;    // instance and pins; baud 0 is default_baud
    unsigned int chip_select_gpio = 0;
};

inline constexpr unsigned long default_baud = 20'000'000;

class Chip final : public mm::fs::FlashDevice {
public:
    explicit Chip(Wiring wiring);
    Status geometry(FlashGeometry&);              // 1, 256, 4096, size / 4096
    Status read(std::uint64_t offset, std::span<std::byte>);
    Status program(std::uint64_t offset, std::span<const std::byte>);
    Status erase(std::uint64_t offset, std::uint64_t size);
    Status sync();                                // Ok: nothing is held back
    std::uint32_t jedec_id() const;               // 0 until identified
};

}
Closing fence

The chip is identified on first use, not at construction. Identification
configures SPI in mode 0, most significant bit first, at the wiring's baud,
makes the chip select an output and raises it, sends release power-down
(0xAB) and waits 5 us, reads status register 1 (0x05), waits for BUSY to
clear, and reads the JEDEC ID (0x9F). A status of 0xFF -- a receive line
nobody drives -- or a manufacturer byte of 0x00 or 0xFF is no chip,
TransportError. The capacity byte n gives 2^n bytes; n outside 16 to 24,
64 KiB to 16 MiB, the range three address bytes reach, is Unsupported.

Opening fence
0x9F  JEDEC ID          manufacturer, memory type, capacity
0x05  read status 1     BUSY (bit 0), WEL (bit 1)
0x06  write enable      before every program and erase
0x03  read              24-bit address, any length
0x02  page program      24-bit address, one 256-byte page
0x20  sector erase      4 KiB
0xD8  block erase       64 KiB, where an erase covers a whole aligned block
0xAB  release power-down
Closing fence

read takes any offset and length inside the chip. program takes whole pages
at page-aligned offsets, and erase whole sectors at sector-aligned offsets;
anything else, or a range past the end, is BadArgument before the bus is
touched. Each page program and each erase is: write enable, a status read
that must show WEL, the command with its address and data, chip select
raised, and status reads until BUSY clears -- within 10 ms for a page, 1 s
for a sector, 4 s for a block, each well past the datasheets' maxima -- or
Timeout. Any failure forgets the chip and the next call identifies it again.
Chip select is low only inside a command, so the bus can be shared.

littlefs on a chip uses a block size of 4096, the erase size, which is also
what littlefs-python's images use.

# Tests

tests/mm/linux drives Chip, unmodified, against the emulated W25Q through the
storage-linux bus: geometry and the JEDEC ID, an erase that uses one block
and two sectors and touches exactly its range, programs read back from any
byte, refusals of unaligned and out-of-range arguments, a missing chip
answered at once, and a capacity below 64 KiB. The emulator counts programs
of unerased bits, programs and erases without write enable, and commands sent
while busy; the tests require all three to stay zero, and removing the BUSY
poll after a page program makes them fail.
